# toolchain-1985.list -- the Mark Williams C chain as the machine shipped with
# it, in June 1985.
#
# TWO COMPILERS EXIST IN THIS PROJECT AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME COMPONENT.
# lists/toolchain.list installs `ours' -- the cc0/cc1/cc2 self-host fixpoint
# built from source in the toolchain repository, on /usr, for a machine that is
# meant to compile its own sources.  This list installs the ORIGINAL: twelve
# Mark Williams binaries, on /bin and /lib.  Nothing here is built from source
# anywhere; they are the bytes the machine shipped with and they exist in
# exactly one place, commodore-900-toolchain's vendor/mwc-1985, which is where
# every src= below reaches them.
#
# BOTH COMPILERS COME FROM THE TOOLCHAIN REPOSITORY, and that is the whole of
# the arrangement: a compiler is the toolchain's artifact whether it was built
# from source or recovered, so a distribution names one and stages it, and no
# other repository holds a copy to go stale.
#
# A COMPILER IS SOMETHING A DISTRIBUTION ADDS.  Base is a runtime -- the
# commands, the headers and the data of a system that runs -- and nothing whose
# purpose is to produce a program.  So this is `role optional' and an `include'
# line, the same shape lists/toolchain.list has: ask for a system and you get a
# runtime; ask for a compiler as well and you say so.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE.  The 1985 `as', `ld', `ar', `nm' and `size' sit beside
# these twelve in vendor/mwc-1985 and no image installs them.  An assembler and
# a linker reach an image only through lists/toolchain.list, which stages ours
# at /usr/bin; nothing puts either at /bin.
#
# NO `package' LINE.  A list with one is an addressable unit of a release
# channel (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT); this one is not: the twelve bytes staged
# below are the toolchain repository's own vendor/mwc-1985, and that
# repository packages them itself rather than this one cutting a
# toolchain-1985-man archive over vendor bytes it did not recover and holds no
# terms for.

# --- the driver and the tools around it --------------------------------------
# ccx IS the driver: it execs cpp, the passes, crts0.o and the loader by
# absolute /lib and /bin paths.  cc is a 54-byte shell script in front of it,
# supplying the four -V options this machine's default model wants.  db is the
# debugger, l the l.out lister and nld the loader for the 32-bit object format
# cc2 emits (l_flag carries LF_32).
f /bin/cc                      755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cc
f /bin/ccx                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/ccx
f /bin/db                      755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/db
f /bin/l                       755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/l
f /bin/nld                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/nld

# --- the passes --------------------------------------------------------------
# cpp is the preprocessor and cc0-cc3 the four compiler passes, on /lib because
# that is where the driver looks for them and not on anybody's $PATH.
f /lib/cc0                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cc0
f /lib/cc1                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cc1
f /lib/cc2                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cc2
f /lib/cc3                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cc3
f /lib/cpp                     755 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/cpp

# --- the small-model runtime -------------------------------------------------
# What ccx links against: the startoff and C library of the small model, which
# has no source and no build wiring in any repository here.
f /lib/scrts0.o                644 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/scrts0.o
f /lib/slibc.a                 644 0 1	src=vendor/mwc-1985/slibc.a
# end of toolchain-1985.list
